#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5d76e6-3f57-520c-8785-6b22b083ccab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62\\,236" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312038} {:answer/id #uuid "f54bacca-4a59-5d5c-9358-4466c0cf8879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76\\,234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312039} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf6c092-bfd0-5bb0-a388-ac9a40f4f57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68\\,547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312040} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a2999a-4447-5293-8d88-3595d69f04ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,571" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312041} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d441f2-85ae-5973-b984-defdcc4604b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\,266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d441f2-85ae-5973-b984-defdcc4604b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\,266" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312042} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f32c989-57c6-5f46-90c7-eeb8eb1f948c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "448fe4dd-8417-54f6-89ac-af53b209b5fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-120554" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{8}{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{5}{8})=(-38.682\\,1…)^{∘}≈-{38.682}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{38.682}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={321.318}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={321.318}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={38.682}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={38.682}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/assets/f4ddf01db733d5ee2e73a4b8abb0bec84d9ee2f7be0fea03e99c112456be1c14.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{38.682}^{∘} \\\\  & ={218.682}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=321.318\\cdot 218.682≈70\\,266\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "874b150b-f17a-513b-a9ac-c5d973fa2e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312045} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5e4a7f-b933-5b82-afdc-82404ea473a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312046} {:answer/id #uuid "518a85a8-1002-56e5-8b99-69ab87ed0c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312047} {:answer/id #uuid "00b14e18-4fdd-5481-ba33-5eaec93fd946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312048} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f881e0-5621-5aaa-8933-de2a3c647820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "874b150b-f17a-513b-a9ac-c5d973fa2e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312045} :answer-field/id #uuid "650f0cd2-1ae8-5b15-afac-110eee214dff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93bbf63d-41f5-5cec-8b52-34cf770cf20e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120614" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $5-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c47ee97-45eb-58ff-9d5d-1c6ccc7b39c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c47ee97-45eb-58ff-9d5d-1c6ccc7b39c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312052} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a3d8eea-c858-571b-8fa9-6f400cb4eb0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ede57a4-7213-50e1-886b-ce16dd44e947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ede57a4-7213-50e1-886b-ce16dd44e947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312054} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0b6e19-b0e8-5d34-aeb7-260f8ac5dcaf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50e43e47-1126-5fba-b35e-8bf5dbdcd8f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338723" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-2,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-2 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-5 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{4}{5})≈-0.9273\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+2\\pi =-0.9273+2\\pi ≈5.3559\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=5.3559.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/assets/d8951c588af989cc5a77b213f25a485b8a6e9db1d0f164fc326e65d40c0385c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.9273 \\\\  & ≈4.0689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx≈4.1,\\,x≈5.4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8641005-be09-5778-8cae-46b6ea93cfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114394} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3aa03e-c8d4-5914-afd3-571a424bd12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114395} {:answer/id #uuid "0117cefb-4227-5ca9-933c-fc8330cccfe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114396} {:answer/id #uuid "035c4255-87e8-5351-9d41-d73a4031a330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114397} {:answer/id #uuid "2beeb28f-b360-5998-b928-c3118b855e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3aa03e-c8d4-5914-afd3-571a424bd12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114395} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71f95e0-a2ad-5200-90a7-0f28916e8105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c319e35c-f6f5-5224-8a19-cffd75e260ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-66002" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5730b548-1b99-514f-b20b-7a8ab001777e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312057} {:answer/id #uuid "66f5b60d-4f47-5fbb-9f0b-71d91caf8b14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312058} {:answer/id #uuid "663d74c6-5ad9-5f73-a5f8-60e818eca794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312059} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e9b8b0-9a5f-560e-b099-e80583849921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312060} {:answer/id #uuid "c9bfe897-d897-5230-ae49-fa0892de5a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6e9b8b0-9a5f-560e-b099-e80583849921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312060} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b03399-4e11-5d11-88c2-7fe9c9b164c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d424b3c0-2159-5871-9aa2-10c0ec4d744e" :question/math-academy-id "q-77805" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041597/assets/2e1f1ef6c82e30b5f9a591fca432914562f949a7ed5cabc3c4eb92bfecf899ee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is not in the required domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}]}
